<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zerbst</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zerbst is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>. From 1603 to 1793 it was the residence of the princes of Anhalt-Zerbst, who built a large baroque palace there. Its best-known daughter of the house was Sophie Auguste Friederike, who became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. The palace and much of the old town were destroyed in an air raid in April 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zerbst.jpg" alt="Zerbst" width="320" height="261" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: mazbln · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zerbst_Bartholom%C3%A4ikirche.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Zerbst lies in the district of Anhalt-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bitterfeld">Bitterfeld</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a>. Its town centre stands on the river Nuthe about 13 km northeast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>, halfway between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a>. Until an administrative reform in 2007 it was the capital of the former district of Anhalt-Zerbst. In the 8th century the area east of the Elbe was settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polabian-slavs">Polabian Slavs</a>, and the town was named Serbst after them, which developed into Zerbst.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area was incorporated about 937 into the Gau Ciervisti of the Saxon Eastern March in the course of the German eastward settlement. It is not clear when Zerbst was founded, but the name Ciervisti, mentioned as early as 949, may already refer to a fortified Slavic settlement. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thietmar-of-merseburg">Thietmar of Merseburg</a> recorded a town as Zirwisti urbs in 1018. In the early 12th century the Ascanian ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-the-bear">Albert the Bear</a> had the fortress rebuilt, and the settlement was first fortified with town walls about 1250.</p><p>In 1307 Prince Albert I of Anhalt acquired the town from the Barby comital family, beginning centuries of rule by the House of Anhalt. In 1375 Zerbster Bitterbier was first mentioned, and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the town had 600 breweries.</p><p>After the Reformation Zerbst became a Calvinist centre, and the Francisceum Gymnasium Illustre was an important Calvinist college from 1582 to 1798. From 1603 to 1793 it was the residence of the princes of Anhalt-Zerbst, whose rule also included the Lordship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jever">Jever</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-frisia">East Frisia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> composer Johann Friedrich Fasch lived there from 1722 to 1758 as court composer and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapellmeister">Kapellmeister</a>, and Fasch Festivals have been held in the city since 1983. In 1745 Princess Sophie Auguste Friederike of Anhalt-Zerbst married Peter, the heir to the Russian throne. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine II</a> she herself reigned as empress from 1762 to 1796. In 1797 Zerbst became part of the Principality of Anhalt-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In the later part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> a labour camp was set up at the edge of the military airfield, where about 700 inmates classed as part Jewish in Nazi terms did hard labour. On 16 April 1945 some eighty percent of Zerbst was destroyed in an Allied air raid. The old town was rebuilt in the following decades, which fundamentally changed the townscape, as only a few historical structures were preserved or reconstructed. The town, with about 24,000 inhabitants, covers a large area after the incorporation of 21 communities in 2010.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zerbst” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Jever passed to Oldenburg in 1575, in 1667 to Anhalt-Zerbst, and in 1793 to Catherine the Great of Russia, a <b>Zerbst</b> princess.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-jever">Lordship of Jever</a></li><li><blockquote>Anhalt was repeatedly split among branches, such as Aschersleben, Bernburg, Köthen and <b>Zerbst</b>, each ruling small towns and farmland along the Elbe and Saale.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-anhalt">Principality of Anhalt</a></li><li><blockquote>Rudolf, a son of Joachim Ernst, received <b>Zerbst</b> in the 1603 partition of Anhalt.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-anhalt-zerbst">Principality of Anhalt-Zerbst</a></li></ul></section>
